.TH NTPDATE 8 "August 2026" ReBSD
.SH NAME
ntpdate \- set the system clock from an NTP server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ntpdate
[
.B \-qv
] [
.B \-t
.I seconds
]
.I server
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ntpdate
sends an SNTP request through the machine-independent IPv4/UDP stack,
validates the reply, calculates the standard four-timestamp clock offset,
and steps the system clock with
.BR settimeofday (2).
The common TODR layer then writes the new UTC value to every writable
hardware clock attached to the system.
.PP
The server may be an IPv4 address or a host name.
NTP era reconstruction uses the 64-bit ReBSD
.I time_t
and accepts timestamps after the NTP rollover in 2036.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-q
Query and print the result without changing the clock.
.TP
.B \-v
Print the protocol version and server stratum.
.TP
.BI \-t " seconds"
Wait this many seconds for a reply.  The default is 5 seconds.
.SH FILES
.I /var/run/resolv.conf
is normally populated by
.BR dhclient (8)
and used for server-name lookup.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR date (1),
.BR settimeofday (2),
.BR dhclient (8)
